## Chapter 3 HBT Interferometry

HBT interferometry (named after the radio astronomer Robert Hanbury-Brown and mathematician Richard Twiss) is the study of waveform intensity interference, be it from optical, electromagnetic, or quantum waves. It differs from standard Michelson interferometry, which examines the interference of the amplitudes of waves impinging on a single location.

### 3.1 Early Work

The history of HBT begins in astrophysics when, in the late 1950s, Robert Hanbury-Brown and Richard Twiss began to experiment with the idea of measuring the size of stars from the interference in the intensities of radio waves coming from opposite edges of the star to two detectors (radio telescopes) [18]. Typical radio interferometry involves the interference pattern set up when two sources of electromagnetic radiation interfere at a single point far from the source. This type of interference occurs due to a phase or path difference between the amplitudes of two incoming light waves. In intensity interferometry, two detectors measure the light arriving from two separate emission points on the star (for many different emission point pairs). Since the light waves consist of photons, which are indistinguishable, there are actually two possibleroutes the light waves can traverse in order to reach the detectors on Earth. This uncertainty means that both possibilities must be used when defining the state of the photons, and it is this combination which imparts a correlation, which in turn gives a measure of the star's size.

#### Radio Astronomy

Michelson interferometry was first used (and ironically, is still used) to determine the apparent angular diameter of stars. The setup, shown in Figure 3.1, consists of two receivers at some distance apart, called the _baseline_. When the star is directly between the two receivers, the path difference between the incoming light waves to the two detectors is zero, and so except for the phase difference, the waves interfere constructively producing a maximum. As the earth rotates, the path difference changes, and eventually the waves destructively interfere to produce a minimum. These minima and maxima are reproduced until the noise overwhelms the system, and the resulting interference pattern is used to find the angular diameter of the source.

The separation of the interference fringes (and thus the resolving power of the interferometer) is inversely dependent on the length of the baseline and the diameter of the star. Resolving power is higher when the fringes are close together, so the obvious way to increase resolution would be to increase the baseline. This however became a significant technical problem during the time Hanbury Brown and Twiss were making their measurements, since there was no reliable way of transmitting phase information across very large distances. In order to circumvent this limitation, they decided to use a method which would not require phase stability, but rather use the wave intensity.

In Figure 3.2 a schematic is shown of the HBT intensity interferometer. Here, waves emitted from sources \({\bf a}\) and \({\bf b}\) impinge on detectors \({\bf 1}\) and \({\bf 2}\). The resulting amplitudes seen at each detector are given by:

\[A_{1} =\frac{1}{L}\left(\alpha e^{i(pr_{1a}+\phi_{a})}+\beta e^{i(pr_{1b} +\phi_{b})}\right)\] \[A_{2} =\frac{1}{L}\left(\alpha e^{i(pr_{2a}+\phi_{a})}+\beta e^{i(pr_{2b }+\phi_{b})}\right), \tag{3.1}\]

where \(\alpha\) and \(\beta\) represent the emission strengths at source points \({\bf a}\) and \({\bf b}\), respectively, and \(\phi_{a}\) and \(\phi_{b}\) are the starting phases of the two waves as they are emitted from the two source points. In this example, both waves are assumed to have been emitted with the same momentum \(p\).

The difference between Michelson and HBT interferometry comes about in how the intensities measured at detectors \({\bf 1}\) and \({\bf 2}\),

\[I_{1} =|A_{1}|^{2} =\frac{1}{L^{2}}\left(|\alpha|^{2}+|\beta|^{2}+2Re\left[\alpha^{ *}\beta e^{i[p(r_{1b}-r_{1a})+(\phi_{b}-\phi_{a})]}\right]\right)\] \[I_{2} =|A_{2}|^{2} =\frac{1}{L^{2}}\left(|\alpha|^{2}+|\beta|^{2}+2Re\left[\alpha^{ *}\beta e^{i[p(r_{2b}-r_{2a})+(\phi_{b}-\phi_{a})]}\right]\right), \tag{3.2}\]

Figure 3.1: Diagram of a Michelson interferometer used to detect the angular diameter of a radio source.

are used in the experiment. If the intensity is examined for a short time, the initial interference pattern will show itself due to the phase variation in the last term of equation (3.2). If the intensity is averaged over long times, these phases cancel due to the randomness in \(\phi_{a}\) and \(\phi_{b}\), and the interference fringes seen at the single detectors vanish:

\[\langle I_{1}\rangle=\langle I_{2}\rangle=\frac{1}{L^{2}}\left(\left|\alpha \right|^{2}+\left|\beta\right|^{2}\right). \tag{3.3}\]

Examination of the coincidence rate, \(\langle I_{1}I_{2}\rangle\), again averaged over long times, gives the product of the two single intensity measurements at the two detectors, plus an extra term which depends on the physical extent of the source. Normalizing the coincidence rate by the product of the single intensities gives the correlation function,

Figure 3.2: HBT intensity interferometry diagram. Waves emitted from source points **a** and **b** can go two possible routes to detectors **1** and **2**.

a direct link between the property of the source that is to be measured, namely its physical extent, and the observed data, or the measured intensities at two detectors:

\[C\left(\overrightarrow{R},\overrightarrow{d}\right)=\frac{\left\langle I_{1}I_{2 }\right\rangle}{\left\langle I_{1}\right\rangle\left\langle I_{2}\right\rangle} =1+\frac{2\left|\alpha\right|^{2}\left|\beta\right|^{2}}{\left(\left|\alpha \right|^{2}+\left|\beta\right|^{2}\right)^{2}}Cos\left[p\left(r_{1a}-r_{2a}-r_ {1b}+r_{2b}\right)\right]. \tag{3.4}\]

Equation (3.4) demonstrates that correlations still exist even in time-averaged intensity measurements. Although this would (temporarily) prove useful in the astronomical measurements made by Hanbury Brown and Twiss, its most consequential impact would be in the realm of particle physics. Eventually, techniques were developed that allowed the amplitudes from widely spaced radio telescopes to be combined without loss of phase information, and Michelson interferometry soon emerged again as the primary method of determining angular diameters of stellar objects.

#### Pion Correlations from the Bevatron

In 1959 measurements at the Bevatron showed a propensity for like-signed pions to be measured at a smaller relative angle than was ascribed by energy-momentum conservation and Fermi statistics [19]. At the time it was believed that the discrepancy was due to "other pion-pion correlations", and one year later the foundation for HBT as it is used today was laid by identifying Bose-Einstein statistics as the major contributor to the Bevatron results [20].

Under the rules of Bose-Einstein statistics, two pions emitted from two separate locations in the source can arrive at two detectors, exactly as in the astronomical case of Figure 3.2. Since the pions, being bosons, are indistinguishable, their wavefunctionis a superposition of the two possible paths for the pions to follow [20]:

\[\phi^{S}\left(1,2\right)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left[e^{i\mathbf{p_{1}\cdot r_{1}}}e^ {i\mathbf{p_{2}\cdot r_{2}}}+e^{i\mathbf{p_{1}\cdot r_{2}}}e^{i\mathbf{p_{2} \cdot r_{1}}}\right]. \tag{3.5}\]

Here, \(\mathbf{r}_{1}\) and \(\mathbf{r}_{2}\) are the distances between the source and detectors \(\mathbf{1}\) and \(\mathbf{2}\), respectively. Integrating the square of \(\phi^{S}\left(1,2\right)\) gives the probability distribution of simultaneously finding two pions with momenta \(\mathbf{p_{1}}\) and \(\mathbf{p_{2}}\). Normalizing this distribution by the volume (assumed spherical) of the source results in the correlation function:

\[\psi\left(1,2\right)=1+9\left(\frac{\cos t}{t^{2}}-\frac{\sin t}{t^{3}}\right) ^{2},t=\left|\mathbf{p_{1}}-\mathbf{p_{2}}\right|\rho, \tag{3.6}\]

where \(\rho\) represents radius of the integration volume. Just as in equation (3.4), the pion correlation function is fit to experimental data, in this case the \(t\) distribution of pion pair momenta, and a parameter related to the size of the interaction volume, \(\rho\), is extracted.

Although the formalism used here is useful to develop an intuitive understanding of HBT, in the next section a more comprehensive analysis is used employing pion field operators. This analysis lends itself to a simpler understanding of the main topic of this thesis, three-pion HBT correlations.

### Theory of Identical Boson Interferometry

Pions emitted from a heavy-ion collision behave according to the QED equation of state for free-traveling particles, the Klein-Gordon equation:

\[\left(\frac{\partial^{2}}{\partial t^{2}}+\nabla^{2}+m_{\pi}^{2}\right)\Phi \left(x\right)=J\left(x\right). \tag{3.7}\]In this equation, \(J\left(x\right)\) represents the nuclear current operator acting as the source of pions [21], \(m_{\pi}\) is the mass of a pion and \(\Phi\left(x\right)\) is the pion field. If pion-nucleon rescattering is ignored, then the pion and nuclear field equations can be decoupled from equation (3.7), and the problem is reduced to solving for the pion field. The single and two-particle momentum distributions are calculated directly from the resulting field equations, providing a link between the experimentally observed distributions and the source dynamics at freeze-out.

#### Pion field Operators

The single and two-particle coincidence momentum probability distributions for pions are given below:

\[P_{1}\left(p\right)= E_{p}\frac{dN}{d^{3}p} =E_{p}\left\langle a_{p}^{\dagger}a_{p}\right\rangle \tag{3.8}\] \[P_{2}\left(p_{1},p_{2}\right)= E_{p_{1}}E_{p_{2}}\frac{dN}{d^{3}p_{1}d^{3}p_{2}} =E_{p_{1}}E_{p_{2}}\left\langle a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{p_{2}}^{ \dagger}a_{p_{2}}a_{p_{1}}\right\rangle. \tag{3.9}\]

In the above equation, \(E_{p}\) is the energy of a pion with momentum \(p\), and \(a_{p}^{\dagger},a_{p}\) represent the creation and annihilation operators acting on a coherent state \(\left|J\right\rangle\), the solution to equation (3.7). One of the properties of \(\left|J\right\rangle\) is that it is an eigenstate of \(a_{p}\), so that

\[a_{p}\left|J\right\rangle=iJ\left(p\right)\left|J\right\rangle. \tag{3.10}\]

Equation (3.10) allows an immediate computation of the the probability distributions in equations (3.8) and (3.9). The two-pion _correlation function_\(C\left(p_{1},p_{2}\right)\) is the experimentally measured ratio of the two-pion coincidence momentum distribution to the single pion distribution, and for the above coherent state \(\left|J\right\rangle\) it is found to be:

\[C\left(p_{1},p_{2}\right)=\frac{P_{2}\left(p_{1},p_{2}\right)}{P_{1}\left(p_{ 1}\right)P_{1}\left(p_{2}\right)}=\frac{\left\langle a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{p_ {2}}^{\dagger}a_{p_{2}}a_{p_{1}}\right\rangle}{\left\langle a_{p_{1}}^{ \dagger}a_{p_{1}}\right\rangle\left\langle a_{p_{2}}^{\dagger}a_{p_{2}}\right\rangle }=\frac{\left|J\left(p_{1}\right)\right|^{2}\left|J\left(p_{2}\right)\right|^{2 }}{\left|J\left(p_{1}\right)\right|^{2}\left|J\left(p_{2}\right)\right|^{2}}=1 \tag{3.11}\]Thus, for pions behaving under the nuclear current operator in equation (3.7), there are no momentum correlations between pairs. In order to calculate the effect of pions emitted from a chaotic source, it is necessary to use a superposition of classical sources distributed in phase space. By averaging over these sources, the two-particle momentum probability distribution (Equation 3.9) can be factorized as [22]:

\[P_{2}\left(p_{1},p_{2}\right)=E_{p_{1}}E_{p_{2}}\left\langle a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{p_{2}}^{\dagger}a_{p_{2}}a_{p_{1}}\right\rangle=E_{p_{1}}E_{p_{2}}\left[ \left\langle a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{p_{1}}\right\rangle\left\langle a_{2}^{ \dagger}a_{p_{2}}\right\rangle+\left\langle a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{p_{2}} \right\rangle\left\langle a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{p_{1}}\right\rangle\right]. \tag{3.12}\]

This factorization assumes independent particle emission, or full source chaoticity. It is one of the central assumptions of HBT, and testing it will be the primary focus of Chapter 4. Another name for the factorization in equation (3.12) is the generalized Wick Theorem. Heuristically it can be seen as a description of the two possible paths two identical particles can take after being emitted from two source points and arriving at two detection points. The Wick Theorem will be expanded for the three-particle case in the next chapter.

Using equation (3.12), the two-pion correlation function can be calculated for a chaotic source:

\[C_{2}\left(p_{1},p_{2}\right)=\frac{P_{2}\left(p_{1},p_{2}\right)}{P_{1}\left( p_{1}\right)P_{1}\left(p_{2}\right)}=1+\frac{\left|\left\langle a_{p_{1}}^{ \dagger}a_{p_{2}}\right\rangle\right|^{2}}{\left\langle a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{ p_{1}}\right\rangle\left\langle a_{p_{2}}^{\dagger}a_{p_{2}}\right\rangle}. \tag{3.13}\]

The expectation values can be computed and related to the Wigner phase-space density, \(S\left(x,K\right)\), the quantum mechanical analogue of the classical phase-space density [23] which is a function of both space \(x\) and total momentum \(K\). The resulting expression is [24]:

\[\sqrt{E_{p_{i}}E_{p_{j}}}\left\langle a_{p_{i}}^{\dagger}a_{p_{j}}\right\rangle =\int S\left(x,K_{ij}\right)e^{iq_{ij}x}d^{4}x. \tag{3.14}\]In this equation, \(K_{ij}=\frac{p_{i}+p_{j}}{2}\) is the average momentum of a pair of pions \(i\) and \(j\), and \(q_{ij}=\left|p_{i}-p_{j}\right|\) is the invariant momentum difference of the pair. Using equation (3.14) with equation (3.13) gives the expression for the two-pion correlation function in terms of the Wigner phase-space density:

\[C_{2}\left(p_{1},p_{2}\right)=1+\frac{\left|\int d^{4}xS\left(x,K\right)e^{iq \cdot x}\right|^{2}}{\int d^{4}xS\left(x,p_{1}\right)\int d^{4}xS\left(x,p_{2} \right)}. \tag{3.15}\]

In order to parameterize the two-pion correlation function in terms of the physical observables of pion momenta, the Wigner phase-space emission function \(S\left(x,K\right)\) can be approximated by a Gaussian distribution [25]. This results in a Gaussian correlation function in terms of the momentum difference \(\mathbf{q}=\mathbf{p_{1}}-\mathbf{p_{2}}\):

\[C_{2}\left(\mathbf{q},\mathbf{K}\right)=1+e^{-q_{\mu}q_{\nu}\langle\tilde{x}^ {\mu}\tilde{x}^{\nu}\rangle\left(\mathbf{K}\right)}. \tag{3.16}\]

\(\tilde{x}^{\mu}\) describes the rms widths of effective sources of particles with total momentum \(\mathbf{K}\). Different parameterizations can obtain these widths in a particular coordinate system to describe the size of the freeze-out region, however the widths obtained only describe the length of a "region of homogeneity" where particle pairs with momentum \(\mathbf{K}\) are emitted.

#### Correlation Function Parameterizations

\(\mathbf{Q}_{inv}\)

The simplest Gaussian parameterization folds all length and time information into one length scale, extracted from a distribution of invariant four-momentum differences (\(Q_{inv}=\sqrt{\left|\mathbf{p_{1}}-\mathbf{p_{2}}\right|^{2}-\left(E_{1}-E_{ 2}\right)^{2}}\)). Once the correlation function has been computed, it is fit to the following equation:

\[C_{2}\left(\mathbf{q},\mathbf{K}\right)=1+\lambda\left(\mathbf{K}\right)e^{-Q _{inv}^{2}\cdot R_{inv}^{2}\left(\mathbf{K}\right)}. \tag{3.17}\]The \(\lambda\) parameter in equation (3.17) represents the degree of contamination in the particle sample being studied, as well as a measure of the degree of chaoticity in the system. In a pure particle sample with full chaoticity its value would be unity. Since the correlation function is one-dimensional, very few statistics are needed to obtain a high quality fit to the data, however since the fit parameter \(R_{inv}\left(\mathbf{K}\right)\) folds so much information into one variable, it is difficult draw a substantial conclusion about the properties of the source.

**Bertsch-Pratt Parameterization**

A 3-dimensional cartesian parameterization, first used by S. Pratt and G. Bertsch [26, 27], uses a coordinate system based on the pair momentum \(\mathbf{k}\). Looking at Figure 3.3, for every pair, the momentum of each track is projected along the beam direction and the transverse direction. The transverse momenta are further split into two components, one in the direction of \(\mathbf{k_{T}}\) of the pair and the other in the orthogonal direction. From these projections, the invariant momentum difference is calculated for each direction: \(\mathbf{k_{T}}\) (out); \(\bot\) to \(\mathbf{k_{T}}\) (side); and parallel to the beam (long). These quantities are then used to produce a 3-dimensional correlation function, which can be fit to:

\[C_{2}\left(\mathbf{q},\mathbf{K}\right)=1+\lambda\left(\mathbf{K}\right)e^{- q_{out}^{2}\cdot R_{out}^{2}\left(\mathbf{K}\right)-q_{side}^{2}\cdot R_{side}^{2} \left(\mathbf{K}\right)-q_{long}^{2}\cdot R_{long}^{2}\left(\mathbf{K} \right)}. \tag{3.18}\]

Although all three radius parameters describe lengths of homogeneity along the three axes, \(R_{out}\left(\mathbf{K}\right)\) contains additional information about the lifetime of the source, due to the fact that this parameter is sensitive to pions which are created at a later time, traveling in the direction of \(\mathbf{k}\) of the pion pair, whereas \(R_{side}\left(\mathbf{K}\right)\) is sensitive only to those pions created at the periphery of the freeze-out region, at the onset of hadronization, and not to those created at later time.

The difference in sensitivity between \(R_{out}\left(\mathbf{K}\right)\) and \(R_{side}\left(\mathbf{K}\right)\) to the pions with delayed emission allows in a simplified picture for an estimate of the lifetime of the freeze-out source [25]:

\[R_{out}^{2}\left(\mathbf{K}\right)-R_{side}^{2}\left(\mathbf{K}\right)\approx \beta_{\perp}^{2}\left\langle\!\left.\tilde{t}^{2}\right\rangle. \tag{3.19}\]

In this equation, \(\beta_{\perp}^{2}\) is the transverse velocity of pair, and \(\left\langle\!\left.\tilde{t}^{2}\right\rangle\right\) is the average emission duration of the source. Estimating the lifetime with equation (3.19) assumes that \(R_{out}\left(\mathbf{K}\right)\) and \(R_{side}\left(\mathbf{K}\right)\) measure approximately the same physical homogeneity length of the source. This can be a faulty assumption if the source is opaque (See figure 3.4), in which case \(R_{side}\left(\mathbf{K}\right)\) would measure a much larger homogeneity length than \(R_{out}\left(\mathbf{K}\right)\), resulting in an imaginary emission lifetime [28].

